      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle fundamentally establishes a limit on the precision with which certain pairs of complementary physical properties of a particle, such as position ($\\Delta x$) and momentum ($\\Delta p$), can be known simultaneously. Mathematically, this is expressed as the inequality $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$, where $\\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant. This constraint is not a limitation of our measuring instruments, but a deep, intrinsic property of quantum mechanics itself, arising from the wave-particle duality of matter. Attempting to precisely localize a particle necessarily increases the uncertainty in its momentum, and vice versa.",
