      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The most profound way to understand the Uncertainty Principle is through the wave-packet perspective, which ties the uncertainty directly to the Fourier transform relationship between position and momentum eigenstates. A precisely known momentum corresponds to a pure plane wave ($\\psi(x) = e^{ikx}$), which has perfectly defined momentum but is infinitely spread out across all space, meaning $\\Delta x = \\infty$. Conversely, a perfectly localized particle requires a superposition of many different momentum states, represented by a sharply peaked wave packet, which inevitably broadens the momentum distribution, resulting in a large $\\Delta p$. This mathematical consequence of the Fourier transform dictates the minimum non-zero uncertainty product because these two spreads, when viewed in reciprocal space, are fundamentally linked by the transform kernel itself, ensuring the inequality $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$ holds for any physically realizable localized wave function.",
      "source": "target",
